{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_vngto374ux3reakra37k5slt3j755jwhihg3ae7yz23reap6eo4a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Translation complete. Summary:\n\n- **Cargo.toml**: Configured as a `cdylib` library with `libloading` and `libc` as dev dependencies.\n- **src/lib.rs**: Translated `driver.c`. The key insight was that glibc's `<ctype.h>` defines `isalnum`, `isdigit`, etc. as **inline macros** that expand to `(*__ctype_b_loc())[c] & MASK`, returning the raw bitmask value (e.g., `2048`), not the 0/1 value the linkable function symbol returns. Likewise `tolower`/`toupper` are defined inline as table lookups via `__ctype_tolower_loc()` / `__ctype_toupper_loc()`. To get byte-identical output, the Rust code calls these glibc internal locale-table accessors directly, matching the macro expansion exactly.\n- **tests/ffi_compare.rs**: Loads both `c_src/build/libdriver.so` and `target/release/libdriver.so` via `libloading`, captures stdout via pipe+dup2, and asserts byte-for-byte equality across all 256 byte values (ASCII letters, digits, punctuation/whitespace, control chars, and high bytes).\n- **Symbols**: Both `.so` files export `driver` as a `T` symbol; no other application-level symbols exist in the C library.\n- **Result**: All 5 test cases pass, covering the full byte range."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":540,"cache_read_input_tokens":34144,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":540,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"24c30404-16a7-4a8f-ac4a-6fb15de66248","uuid":"b23029c5-52e1-46db-b4ef-0369d970a8b9"}
